The developer-productivity agent is built directly on the Claude API so the team can log every round trip. Claude has just returned a response with stop_reason "tool_use" containing:

    {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01Xy9", "name": "read_module", "input": {"path": "billing/ledger.py"}}

The team executes the read and now has to hand the file contents back. Which message correctly returns the result?

After executing process_refund the returns workflow appends this message to the conversation:

    {"role": "user", "content": [
      {"type": "text", "text": "Here is the refund result."},
      {"type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Ab7", "content": "refund_id=RF-8812 status=posted"}
    ]}

Every request now fails with a 400 error reading "tool_use ids were found without tool_result blocks immediately after". Which change to that user message resolves the error?

A PreToolUse hook is meant to redact a raw card number from the `notes` field before `process_refund` runs. It returns:

```
return {
  hookSpecificOutput: { hookEventName: "PreToolUse", permissionDecision: "defer" },
  updatedInput: { ...input, notes: redact(input.notes) }
};
```

In production the unredacted value still reaches the tool, and the run stops before the agent has finished the case -- not with an error, but in a state the supervising application is expected to pick up and resume. What explains both symptoms?

A platform team commits this `.mcp.json` to the repository so every engineer picks up the internal documentation server:

```
{"mcpServers": {"docs": {
  "type": "http",
  "url": "https://mcp.${REGION:-eu}.acme.internal/docs",
  "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer ${DOCS_TOKEN}"}
}}}
```

Which TWO statements about this configuration are correct? (Select TWO)

An extraction workflow retrieves internal policy documents through a custom retrieval tool declared in the request's `tools` array, and returns them from the tool's `tool_result` as content blocks shaped like this:

{"type": "search_result", "source": "kb://policy-1187", "title": "Returns Policy 2026", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Refunds are issued to the original payment method within 14 days."}]}

The extracted output is accurate, but the response carries no citation blocks at all, so downstream consumers cannot see which policy a value came from. Why?
